libgtk-4-1
gir1.2-gtk-4.0
python3-gi
libnotify-bin
libadwaita-1-0
libgtk4-layer-shell0
libgee-0.8-2
libpeas-2-0
libjson-glib-1.0-0
libsoup-3.0-0
libvte-2.91-gtk4-0
libgtksourceview-5-0
libwebkitgtk-6.0-4
libgraphene-1.0-0
# The payload links these directly; keep them explicit because the current
# package artifact predates correct shlib dependency generation.
libtinysparql-3.0-0
libupower-glib3
libdbusmenu-glib4
libsodium26
upower
gsettings-desktop-schemas
libglib2.0-bin

# Libraries the 2026-10-07 payload links that no other seed pulled in. The
# offline mirror is built from this seed only (build/build-desktop-mirror.sh does
# not read the deb's Depends), so without these `apt-get install
# ncz-singularity-desktop` could not be satisfied from file:///cdrom. Gate:
# build/verify-singularity-depends-seeded.sh. libsane1 and libges-1.0-0 pull
# their own closure (libsane-common, libieee1284, libsnmp, libnl-route, ...).
libfreerdp3-3
libfreerdp-client3-3
libwinpr3-3
libges-1.0-0
libsane1
libsfdo0

# --- Wayland session infra --------------------------------------------------
foot
xsettingsd
seatd
libseat1
libliftoff0
libinput10
libxcb-composite0
libxcb-ewmh2
libxcb-icccm4
libxcb-res0
# labwc links libxcb-errors for readable X11 protocol errors under Xwayland.
libxcb-errors0
swaybg
grim
slurp
# zenity backs ncz-screenshot-timer's delay-entry/countdown dialog (see
# post-install/20-desktop.sh) -- a portal-independent screenshot path.
# xdg-desktop-portal's Screenshot request hangs (GTask never returns) even
# after the singularity.portal/wlr-fallback staging fixes below, so both
# gnome-screenshot and Singularity's own screenshot utility fail; grim+
# slurp called directly, with no portal involved, is what actually works
# -- confirmed live on O6N 2026-08-25.
zenity
fuzzel
xwayland
polkitd
# PipeWire/WirePlumber request realtime scheduling through rtkit.  Keep this
# explicit: 20-desktop.sh installs it in the same transaction as the desktop,
# so one missing candidate prevents every package in that transaction from
# being installed on the offline target.
rtkit
xdg-desktop-portal

# --- system bus --------------------------------------------------------------
# dbus/dbus-broker are base-layer services. Desktop components depend on the
# already-present system bus and do not seed the bus owner themselves.

# --- AppArmor (NCZ-OS 26.7 audit W3.5) --------------------------------------
# Mandatory access control via AppArmor. The kernel config
# (assets/kernel/edge/config-7.2.0-rc7-sky1-ncz, verified at audit time):
#   CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR=y
#   CONFIG_DEFAULT_SECURITY_APPARMOR=y
#   CONFIG_LSM="landlock,lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,integrity,apparmor,selinux,smack,tomoyo,bpf,ipe"
# i.e. AppArmor is built AND is the default LSM (no lsm= cmdline override
# needed). Bundled profiles load at boot in COMPLAIN mode by default;
# post-install/92-apparmor-enable.sh enables apparmor.service but does
# NOT flip any profile to enforce. Per Debian's apparmor-profiles
# package description, the bundled profiles are not mature enough to
# be shipped in enforce mode by default — that is a deliberate
# policy, not an oversight. Do NOT write release notes claiming
# shipped profiles are in enforce mode; they are not.
#
# apparmor-profiles pulls the upstream Debian profile bundle
# (usr.sbin.*, abstractions/, tunables/). apparmor-utils ships the
# `aa-complain` / `aa-enforce` / `aa-disable` admin CLI; not strictly
# required for boot, but operators will want them to promote
# individual tested profiles to enforce mode on a per-service basis
# (aa-enforce <profile>) rather than blanket-enforcing the whole
# bundle.
#
# The systemd unit (apparmor.service) loads shipped profiles at boot.
# post-install/92-apparmor-enable.sh enables that unit and logs
# `aa-status` so a future audit can see exactly which profiles are
# loaded and in what mode.
apparmor
apparmor-profiles
apparmor-utils

# post-install/34-firewall.sh installs and validates the nftables policy during
# target setup. Seed the package explicitly so that install is offline-safe;
# the hook's apt-get fallback must not depend on live Debian access.
nftables

# systemd-zram-generator is the userspace half of W3.6 (zram swap).
# The kernel half (CONFIG_ZRAM=y, CONFIG_CRYPTO_ZSTD=y) must be set
# in the CIX Sky1 kernel build — see post-install/93-zram-swap.sh
# for the runtime check that proves the kernel supports zram.
# Without CONFIG_ZRAM in the kernel, the generator creates no
# devices and this package is a no-op until the kernel gains the
# option. Verified at audit time that the shipped CIX Sky1 kernel
# config (assets/kernel/edge/config-7.2.0-rc7-sky1-ncz) has
# `# CONFIG_ZRAM is not set` and no CONFIG_CRYPTO_ZSTD — i.e. the
# kernel half is MISSING. We ship the userspace anyway so a future
# kernel rebuild with the option enabled gives us zram for free.
systemd-zram-generator

# --- native Wayland screensaver chooser / idle-lock --------------------------
# LIFTED 2026-09-30: the screensaver subsystem now ships; the
# package ships 84 native GLES3 hacks (Black Hole, hyprsaver, xshadertoy, the
# twelve classics) with no OpenGL 1 or gl4es code left in the ncz-screensavers
# package; 59-ncz-screensavers-pkg installs the shipped 74-title catalogue.
# 57-screensaver.sh was a Wayland idle-lock fallback that post-install/59
# fully supersedes on desktop variants: the package owns the engine
# (ncz-screensaver), the idle timer (ncz-screensaver-idled.service), the
# dev.ncz.screensaver GSettings schema and the shipped 74-title catalogue.
# Verified against the published 0.8.9 .deb: it ships NO Singularity plugin
# files, NO GTK4 settings app and NO launcher entry -- the plugin is a
# separate ncz-screensavers-plugin package, not yet published (the deb's own
# Description says it is "installed by the NCZ-OS image"). 57-screensaver.sh was already removed from
# build-squashfs-layers.sh / build-baked-rootfs.sh hook loops and from
# run-all.sh's MACHINE_HOOKS_RE regex; this item (fix/screensavers-mandatory
# -install-20261006; rule since 2026-10-05: screensavers distributed as a .deb
# are apt-installed during installation) finally removes
# the hook from the platform-inventory + both generated hook lists.
# swayidle, swaylock, wlopm, and the GObject-introspection packages below
# remain removed -- they have no other consumer (verified by grep against
# post-install/ + preseed/ on 2026-08-21).
# Build dependencies stay for the image assembly chroot for the NCZ Vala UI.
valac
meson
ninja-build
libgtk-4-dev
libgee-0.8-dev

# --- GPU / GL tooling (Mali GLES; Vulkan tools for diagnostics) -------------
mesa-utils
vulkan-tools
vainfo
# The Mesa Vulkan ICDs, which is what gives PANTHOR a Vulkan driver (panvk).
# Without this the image ships the Vulkan loader, the tools and the validation
# layers but no Mesa ICD, so /usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/ is empty and Panthor has
# GL only -- measured on O6N 2026-08-19, where `dpkg -l mesa-vulkan-drivers`
# read `un <none>`. The CIX blob is unaffected either way: it registers its own
# ICD at /etc/vulkan/icd.d/mali.json, a path the loader searches separately.
# Both can be installed at once; vulkaninfo then enumerates each GPU that has a
# bound kernel driver.
mesa-vulkan-drivers
# Mesa's OpenCL ICD is the stock-Debian rusticl path for panthor. It requires
# RUSTICL_ENABLE=panfrost (set by 16-mesa-gpu-2613.sh); without this package
# clinfo reports zero platforms even after Vulkan works.
mesa-opencl-icd
libglu1-mesa
clinfo

# Firmware visibility. fwupd enumerates updatable device firmware (SSD, dock,
# TPM, UEFI dbx) so we learn something is due instead of inferring it from a
# BIOS date. Inert on Sky1 and on T2 Macs -- neither CIX nor Apple publishes to
# LVFS -- but it costs little and reports honestly when there is nothing to do.
fwupd
glmark2-es2-wayland
glmark2-wayland

# --- audio (PipeWire) -------------------------------------------------------
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
# PipeWire SPA plugins that wireplumber loads but the core packages do not
# pull in (Recommends are off). Without libspa-0.2-bluetooth every login logs
# "SPA handle 'api.bluez5.enum.dbus' could not be loaded; is it installed?"
# and Bluetooth audio never appears; without libspa-0.2-libcamera cameras are
# only reachable through the raw V4L2 path. libcamera-ipa carries the image
# processing modules libcamera Recommends (soft ISP for raw sensors).
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
libspa-0.2-libcamera
libcamera-ipa
# pactl is in pulseaudio-utils, and PipeWire Pulse intentionally only
# suggests it. The Sky1 HDMI/DP autoswitch user service needs pactl to load
# isolated module-alsa-sink instances and set the session default sink.
pulseaudio-utils
pavucontrol

# --- removable media + bluetooth --------------------------------------------
# NETWORK: sinty-nm is installed from the base layer by 32-sinty-nm.sh. Do NOT
# add network-manager or network-manager-gnome here; NetworkManager is only the
# base fallback when the gitignored sinty-nm build blob is absent.
gvfs
gvfs-backends
udisks2
# libmtp-runtime ships /usr/lib/udev/mtp-probe, which libmtp-common's
# 69-libmtp.rules calls for every USB device; without it udev logs "Failed to
# find and pin callout binary /usr/lib/udev/mtp-probe" for each one per boot
# and MTP phones are not tagged for gvfs.
libmtp-runtime
bluez
blueman
# vendor cix-debian-misc's bluetooth-autoconnect script does `import dbus`
# with no declared dependency -- confirmed live on the MS-R1 test board:
# ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'dbus', bluetooth-autoconnect.service
# failed at every boot. python3-dbus is the missing runtime dependency.
python3-dbus

# --- fonts ------------------------------------------------------------------
fonts-noto-core
fonts-noto-cjk
fonts-noto-color-emoji
fonts-liberation
# Inter: Singularity's design face, the default sans-serif (20-desktop.sh
