# --- GTK4/libadwaita native-Wayland apps, hardware-accelerated via GSK's
#     GL renderer (GSK_RENDERER=gl, already set in singularity-desktop-session).
#     kooha fixes the earlier screen-recording bug -- goes through
#     xdg-desktop-portal's ScreenCast interface, which singularity.portal
#     does implement. celluloid is a native GTK4 GUI complement to the CLI
#     mpv already above (same VAAPI hardware decode path). drawing is a
#     small paint app. ---
#
# Stock GNOME apps with a native Singularity twin are NOT installed (operator,
# 2026-10-26: "We shouldn't be installing most of the gnome apps"). Dropped:
#   gnome-calculator -> Calculator     gnome-calendar   -> Calendar
#   gnome-characters -> Characters     gnome-weather    -> Weather
#   gnome-text-editor-> Edit/Notes     gnome-font-viewer-> Fonts
#   gnome-sound-recorder -> Recorder   gnome-console    -> Leafs (terminal)
#   gnome-disk-utility -> Disks        baobab           -> Disk Usage
#   epiphany-browser -> Browser        loupe            -> Photos
#   papers           -> Reader
# Nothing kept depends on them (checked against the pinned snapshot and a real
# install). Default handlers for their types point at the Singularity apps
# (post-install/lib/ncz-default-apps.sh, applied by 59-desktop-curate.sh).
# Two packages that only arrived as their dependencies stay, seeded directly:
#   geoclue-2.0          Singularity Weather, Maps and the shell ask
#                        org.freedesktop.GeoClue2 for the location over D-Bus
#   gnome-desktop3-data  shared GNOME desktop data, kept by operator decision
geoclue-2.0
gnome-desktop3-data
kooha
celluloid
drawing
# Additional Wayland-first utilities: small native image viewers. They do not
# pull in a second desktop session or an Xorg server; Xwayland remains only for
# legacy applications. The terminal is Singularity Leafs (foot stays for the
# scripted NCZ launchers).
imv
swayimg

# zstd: post-install/72-rescue-partition.sh unpacks rescue-rootfs.tar.zst on the
# installed system and hard-requires it. The base layer is the Debian forky
# rootfs tarball, which (unlike Ubuntu) does not ship zstd, and the base layer
# installs no manifest -- so this delta is what puts it on the target. Without
# it 72 aborts, RESCUE_READY is never written, and ncz-refind-refresh refuses
# to install BOOTAA64.EFI, failing the install as unbootable.
zstd

# Generic Linux firmware. Debian splits what Ubuntu ships as one linux-firmware
# package; without these the installed system has only CIX vendor firmware and
# the kernel logs e.g. "Possible missing firmware /lib/firmware/rtl_nic/
# rtl8125k-1.fw for built-in driver r8169". Seeded here so they are in the
# offline mirror closure AND installed into the shipped layer.
# Complete firmware seed: manifests/firmware.pkgs.

# curl + its trust store. Same Debian-vs-Ubuntu gap class as zstd above:
# installer-base.pkgs lists curl, but that manifest only seeds the offline
# MIRROR -- the base layer is the Debian forky rootfs tarball plus hooks and
# installs no manifest, so nothing was actually putting curl on the target.
# Confirmed on O6N 2026-08-02: post-install/30-agents.sh died with
# "curl: command not found" (exit 127) and zoder was never installed; the
# 46-python311.sh network fallbacks are gated on `command -v curl` and were
# silently skipped for the same reason. ca-certificates is required for any
# of those https fetches to validate.
curl
ca-certificates

# systemd-timesyncd. Ubuntu bundles it inside systemd; Debian splits it into
# its own package, so post-install/37-ntp-hostname.sh failed with
# "Failed to enable unit: systemd-timesyncd.service does not exist"
# (confirmed on O6N 2026-08-02). Sky1 boards have no working RTC -- O6N has no
# /dev/rtc at all -- so without a time daemon the clock re-seeds from the image
# build epoch on every boot and TLS certificate validation fails.
systemd-timesyncd
tzdata

# python3-venv / python3-pip. Debian splits venv + ensurepip out of the base
# python3 package (Ubuntu's python3 pulled them in), so post-install/47-embedkit.sh's
# stdlib fallback died with:
#   "You may need to use sudo with that command. After installing the
#    python3-venv package, recreate your virtual environment.
#    Failing command: /opt/ncz/embed-venv/bin/python3"
# and /opt/ncz/embed-venv was never created (reproduced on O6N 2026-08-02:
# `python3 -m venv` fails, python3-venv and python3-pip both absent). The
# preferred path is uv (vendored by build/build-python311-assets.sh), but the
# fallback has to actually work when uv is unavailable.
python3-venv
python3-pip

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARDWARE EXPLOITATION AND WAYLAND/CLI TOOLING (audited on a live r239 install,
# 2026-08-14). Everything below was MISSING from the shipped image while the
# hardware or the desktop stack was already capable of using it.
#
# Wayland session capability
#   xdg-desktop-portal-wlr  Screencast/screenshot portal for wlroots. Without it
#                           SCREEN SHARING CANNOT WORK on this desktop -- labwc is
#                           wlroots-based and only xdg-desktop-portal + -gtk were
#                           installed. PipeWire 1.6.8 is already present, so this
#                           is the one missing link in the chain.
#   wl-clipboard            wl-copy/wl-paste. Baseline Wayland CLI clipboard; the
#                           image shipped with no command-line clipboard at all.
#   wlr-randr               Output/mode configuration from the CLI (4K, multi-head).
#   wf-recorder             Screen recording; can drive the Linlon H.264 encoder.
#   wev                     Input/event debugging -- directly useful on this board,
#                           where USB keyboards need an xhci rebind to enumerate.
#
# Media / VPU
#   gstreamer1.0-tools      gst-launch-1.0 / gst-inspect-1.0. The v4l2 codec plugins
#                           (libgstv4l2codecs.so, libgstvideo4linux2.so) are ALREADY
#                           installed but unusable from the shell without these, and
#                           GStreamer's v4l2h264enc is the alternative to ffmpeg's
#                           v4l2m2m encoder, which SIGSEGVs on teardown (upstream bug).
#
# Hardware diagnostics the box has hardware for
#   nvme-cli                NVMe is the root device; SMART/temperature/namespace.
#   smartmontools           Disk health.
#   ethtool                 10GbE link, ring and offload inspection/tuning.
#   iperf3                  Actually validate that 10GbE.
#   lm-sensors              CLI counterpart to the desktop sensors panel.
#   powertop                Power/idle analysis, relevant to the perf-mode work.
#   sysstat                 iostat/mpstat/sar for performance investigation.
#   btop, htop              Process and resource monitors.
#
# nvme-cli, smartmontools, ethtool and htop were already IN the offline pool as
# transitive dependencies but were never seeded, so they never got installed.
# Seeding them here costs no extra download.
#
# NOTE: the GPU also exposes OpenCL (clinfo reports ARM Platform /
# Mali-G720-Immortalis r0p0). With the NPU unusable (no userspace driver on the
# image at all), OpenCL on the GPU is currently the only working compute path.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
wl-clipboard
wlr-randr
wf-recorder
wev
gstreamer1.0-tools
nvme-cli
smartmontools
ethtool
iperf3
lm-sensors
powertop
sysstat
btop
htop

# --- filesystem tooling for the EFI System Partition ---------------------
# dosfstools was missing from the installed system: mkfs.vfat/fsck.fat are not
# present once d-i finishes, so a shipped machine could not create or repair its
# own ESP -- found on O6N when preparing a UEFI BIOS-update stick, where
# mkfs.vfat simply did not exist. It is already in mgmt.pkgs and rescue.pkgs;
# the installed desktop needs it too.
dosfstools

# --- FFmpeg 9 for the Linlon VPU ------------------------------------------
# Debian's ffmpeg 8.1.2 cannot encode correctly through this hardware: upstream
# decides plane layout from the V4L2 fourcc, so on a multi-planar driver it packs
# every plane into plane 0 -- yuv420p SIGSEGVs and nv12 silently loses all chroma
# (measured U=2 V=2 against U=127 V=126 in software). ncz-ffmpeg carries the fix.
#
# It installs under /opt/ncz-ffmpeg with an RPATH and symlinks ffmpeg/ffprobe/
# ffplay into /usr/local/bin, so it wins on PATH without touching the system
# libav* that the rest of the desktop links against (our soname is .63, forky
# ships .62). The system ffmpeg stays installed for those library consumers.
ncz-ffmpeg

# --- hwclock, so the RTC can actually be written ---------------------------
# Debian moved hwclock OUT of util-linux and into util-linux-extra, which no
# manifest carried -- so the installed desktop had no way to write the RTC at
# all. MEASURED on O6N 2026-08-17: RTC stuck at the firmware default
# 2024-08-30 while the system clock was correct, dmesg seeding the system
# clock from that stale RTC on every boot, and 'hwclock: command not found'.
# Every fresh flash therefore booted ~2 years in the past until NTP won the
# race, breaking TLS-validating tools (apt, curl, git) in the meantime.
#
# post-install/44-rtc-sync.sh does apt-install this as a fallback, but that
# needs working network at install time -- exactly the thing a two-year-old
# clock breaks. Seeding it here keeps the fix OFFLINE-first like the rest of
# this closure.
util-linux-extra

# --- accelerator + audio diagnostics ---------------------------------------
# MEASURED on O6N 2026-08-18: a fully working desktop with no way to inspect
# either subsystem from a shell. `aplay -l` and `v4l2-ctl --info` were the
# first two commands reached for during hardware validation and BOTH were
# missing, on an image whose whole point is CIX Sky1 accelerator support.
#
# alsa-utils is not needed for audio to FUNCTION -- PipeWire drives the
# cix_sky1 card fine without it -- but aplay/amixer/alsamixer/speaker-test are
# how anyone confirms that from the command line, and how a user recovers a
# muted or misrouted card when the GUI mixer is not available.
#
# v4l-utils is the only way to interrogate the Linlon VPU nodes (/dev/video0
# mvxdec, /dev/video1 mvxenc): v4l2-ctl reports the codec formats the firmware
# actually registered, which is exactly the check that distinguishes "VPU
# driver loaded" from "VPU driver loaded AND firmware present". The MS-R1
# test board already had v4l2-ctl, which is why the missing .fwb set was caught there
# first and not here.
alsa-utils
v4l-utils

# --- radio firmware: WiFi/Bluetooth for whatever is in the M.2 slot ---------
# MEASURED 2026-08-18, and this was a real shipping defect: the MS-R1 test board carries a
# MediaTek MT7922 (mt7921e) and its three firmware blobs were present in
# /lib/firmware/mediatek/ -- but `dpkg -S` matched NO PACKAGE for any of them.
# They had been hand-dropped on that box at some point. No manifest carried
# firmware-mediatek, so a CLEAN INSTALL of this image ships zero MediaTek
# firmware and the WiFi is simply dead, on the very board we build on.
#
# It looked fine only because the build host had been hand-patched, which is
# the most dangerous possible way for a gap like this to hide.
#
# The Sky1 boards differ in what sits in the M.2 slot, and Orange Pi 6 is a
# target too, so cover the four families that realistically appear on an ARM
# SBC rather than only the one chip we happen to own:
#   mediatek    MT7902/7921/7922/7925/7961 -- the MS-R1's MT7922
#   brcm80211   Broadcom/AMPAK AP6xxx -- the usual ARM SBC on-board module
#   atheros     QCA6174/QCA9377 M.2 cards
#   iwlwifi     Intel AX200/AX210 M.2 cards
#   ath9k-htc   USB adapters (43 KB, free firmware)
#
# Each package carries BOTH the WiFi and the Bluetooth blobs for its family
# (e.g. BT_RAM_CODE_MT7922_1_1_hdr.bin lives in firmware-mediatek), so this
# also fixes Bluetooth on any board whose BT radio is actually wired up.
#
# Cost: ~99 MB of .deb, ~385 MB installed. A desktop image whose WiFi does not
# come up is broken, so this is not optional weight.
